###### _Occurrence_ 

In titanium, hydride formation occurs by absorption of hydrogen originating from corrosion processes (09.11.20.01) or from hydrogen-containing gas. If more than 150 ppm hydrogen is absorbed, a brittle titanium hydride phase will precipitate. Particularly where the normally present protective oxide skin has been damaged by friction or chemical reduction, the hydrogen absorption by titanium will increase dramatically. Also, the presence of surface contaminants and temperatures above 70°C promote hydrogen absorption. 

